--- NEW
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;
;;
;;  A frame, for code running in the options chunk.
;;
;;    ShopFrame is in this bank, and a routine in another chunk sees its own
;;  bank at $8000 rather than this one -- so the description box cannot just
;;  call it.  JSL lands here with the program bank set to this chunk, the JSR
;;  then reaches ShopFrame normally, and RTL goes back.
;;
;;    Four bytes here save reimplementing the frame, and more to the point
;;  they keep the hand cursor and the party drawn while the box is up.  A
;;  frame assembled out of fixed-bank pieces could not draw either, so the
;;  cursor blinked out and back every time the box opened.
;;
;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;;

.export OptShopFrameFar, OptCallFar
OptShopFrameFar:
    JSR ShopFrame
    RTL

;  The same trick, for any routine in this bank.
;
;  JMP (abs) takes its pointer from bank $00, whose low pages mirror WRAM, so
;  one trampoline serves every routine rather than needing a table of them --
;  which matters, because there are ten bytes left in this bank.  The caller
;  leaves the address in OPTCALLV and A, X and Y pass straight through.
;
;  The data bank has to be set as well as the program bank.  hal_swapprg ends
;  in PLB, so whichever chunk swapped last owns DBR, and the description box
;  leaves it pointing at the options chunk -- a routine in here would then
;  read its own tables out of that chunk and draw nonsense.
OptCallFar:
    PHB
    PHK
    PLB
    JSR @go
    PLB
    RTL
  @go:
    JMP (OPTCALLV)

